The ratio test states:
Let \(x_n
eq 0\) for all \(n\) and let \(L=\displaystyle\lim_{n	o\infty}\left| rac{x_{n+1}}{x_n}ight|\) exist. Then
- If \(L<1\) the series \(\sum x_n\) converges absolutely;
- If \(L>1\) the series diverges;
- If \(L=1\) the test gives no conclusion – the series might converge or diverge. The lack of information occurs because a limit equal to 1 is consistent with both a geometric‑type decay (convergent) and a harmonic‑type behavior (divergent), so the test cannot distinguish between them.

The root test mirrors the ratio test but uses the \(n\)th root. Define \(L=\displaystyle\lim_{n	o\infty}|x_n|^{1/n}\). Then
- If \(L<1\) the series \(\sum x_n\) converges absolutely;
- If \(L>1\) the series diverges;
- If \(L=1\) the test is inconclusive. Like the ratio test, the case \(L=1\) can correspond to either a convergent or a divergent series, so no definitive conclusion can be drawn.

If \(\displaystyle L=\lim_{n	o\infty}\left| rac{x_{n+1}}{x_n}ight|\) exists, then:
 • \(L<1\) ⇒ \(\sum x_n\) converges absolutely;
 • \(L>1\) ⇒ \(\sum x_n\) diverges;
 • \(L=1\) ⇒ the test gives no conclusion.  The lecture cited \(\displaystyle x_n= rac{1}{n}\) (diverges) and \(\displaystyle x_n= rac{1}{n^{2}}\) (converges) as two series for which \(L=1\) but with different outcomes.

The ratio test considers a series \(\sum X_{n}\) with \(X_{n}\neq 0\).  Let \(L = \lim_{n\to\infty}\left|\frac{X_{n+1}}{X_{n}}\right|\).  The conclusions are:

• If \(L<1\), the series converges absolutely;
• If \(L>1\), the series diverges;
• If \(L=1\), the test provides no information—neither convergence nor divergence can be deduced from it.

